  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nCurrently, scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms \ncontinue to propagate east from eastern MT into western ND. \nInstability is limited here and CIN continues to increase over \nwestern ND this afternoon, so the threat for thunderstorms \nremains low here. Over eastern ND, instability is much more \nconcerning with 2500 J/Kg of MLCape situated over the northern \nJames River Valley with little capping and convective \ntemperatures in the low to mid 90s (current temps around 90). \nForecast HRRR soundings indicate a marginally severe potential \nshould convection develop. A limiting factor is building upper \nheights and the lack of forcing for ascent this afternoon, \ncombined with a general drying of the lower boundary layer. \nCams have been back and forth as whether or not to develop \nconvection here today. Currently, a couple of the CAMS are \nshowing at least an attempt at convection, while a majority \nremain dry. If we would see convection from the Turtle Mountains\ninto the James River Valley a stronger to marginally severe \nstorm can not be ruled out. For most, this afternoon and tonight\nwill be dry.\n\nWednesday will be another hot day across western and central \nND. Afternoon high temperatures range mostly from the mid 90s to\nnear 103, with the hottest temperatures over the west central \nand southwest. Heat Advisory criteria may be met, generally in \nareas south and west of the Missouri River. However, the areal \nextent of advisory criteria heat indices is low. There are also \nsome concerns regarding cloud cover from convection, limiting \ntemperatures if it develops earlier in the day. In addition, the\nsouthwest portion of the state will probably see some of the \nlower dewpoint in the area Wednesday afternoon, limiting heat \nindices to below the actual air temperatures. Will let later \nshifts monitor for possible highlights tomorrow. \n\nAnother concern Wednesday will be the threat for severe storms.\nSPC has upgraded pretty much the western half of the state to a\nslight chance (level 2 out of 5) for severe storms. In general,\nit looks like storms will develop over eastern Montana Wednesday\nafternoon and move into western ND through early evening.\nDamaging winds will be possible given the inverted V soundings.\nLarge hail will also be possible given the steep lapse rates \nand increasing winds aloft. We will message the same hazards as \nthe previous forecast, golfball sized hail and 70 mph winds. \nStorm mode looks to be more linear north with bulk shear vectors\nmore parallel to the surface trough, while they are more \nperpendicular south, indicating more of a supercell threat. \nWith a surface low over eastern Montana and strong surface \nheating, there could be a window of opportunity for a tornado \nearly on, in the northwest where LCL heights will be more \nfavorable. Cams are pretty consistent in convection not holding \ntogether as it moves into a less favorable environment over \ncentral ND. This then muddles the picture for potential \nconvection on Thursday. There is currently a marginal risk for \nsevere storms across most of the forecast area on Thursday. We \nwill keep messaging general for Thursday at this time as a lot \nwill depend on how things evolve tomorrow. Bulk shear definitely\nlooks to be weaker and Cape also looks like it may be lower \nwith more cloud cover remaining over a good portion of the \nforecast area. \n\nThursday and Friday will be cooler (relatively) with highs in\nthe mid 80s to lower 90s Thursday, and mid 80s to mid 90s \nFriday. We then warm back up over the weekend with some 100 \ndegree readings possible once again Saturday and especially \nSunday. Heat headlines may once again be in play. Fire weather \nconcerns may also be heightened by Sunday as grasses continue to\ncure and we see stronger southerly winds develop Sunday \nafternoon. Another cooldown does look to be in store for at \nleast the beginning of next week.",
